Issue the consolidated report. Structure exactly as follows.

PAGE 1 — SUMMARY

  How to read it: the weighted score, the Floor rule, the kill gate, the V/I marks — four lines.
  The summary table: rank, name, self-description, weighted score, Floor, kill gate pass/fail, and one line of recommendation per candidate.
  A comparative career timeline showing all candidates on one shared axis, one row each, with time spent doing the core work of the job in one colour and time spent on everything else in another.

PAGE 2 — CONSOLIDATED EVALUATION AND HUMAN-VALIDATED KILL FACTORS
  The scores again, compact.
  Beneath them, the kill factors from Stage 4 as an empty grid: one row per factor, one column per candidate, PASS and FAIL boxes, unticked.
  Beneath that, three rules stated in full:
    One FAIL ends the candidacy, on any single line, regardless of the weighted score.
    A blank is not a pass — unassessed means unknown, and unknown does not clear a gate.
    Both halves must clear. The score measures what the paper supports; the boxes measure what the paper cannot show. Neither substitutes for the other.
  A signature block: interviewer, date, candidate, outcome.

TWO PAGES PER CANDIDATE
  First page — the assessment: weighted score, Floor, kill gate and verdict across the top; the full scoring table; kills; strengths; three questions; recommendation. No timeline on this page.
  Second page — the evidence: that candidate's own career timeline on its own axis; the employer table from Stage 6; and what the research changes for them.

FINAL PAGE — WRAP-UP
  What the pool says as a whole. Whether anyone clears. Where the field splits. Whether the advertisement is recruiting the right discipline. What the research changed. Any correction to something I got wrong. What the real shape of the hire is — one seat, or several.
  Then method and provenance: what the footprint ratings mean, what the financial figures are worth, and the limits of the whole exercise stated plainly.

FORMATTING
  Landscape. One page per section — do not let a candidate's assessment run onto a second page. Check this yourself after producing it, and tighten type and spacing until it holds. Do not hand me a report you have not looked at.
  Per-candidate timelines use each candidate's own span, not a shared one, but the same scale in length per year, so bars stay comparable between pages.
  Laconic throughout. This is read in an interview room, not at a desk.
  Output as both a Word document and a PDF.

IF YOU CANNOT DO THAT
  Go back to the capability list you gave me at the start and substitute, saying which substitution you are making and why:
  - No code execution, so no drawn charts: render each timeline as a monospace bar chart in text, one row per role, with start, end and duration labelled. Do not omit the timeline.
  - No file output: give me the whole report as structured Markdown in the chat, with clear page breaks marked, and I will lay it out.
  - Output length limited: deliver it in labelled parts, in order, saying how many first.
  Never silently drop a section you cannot render. A missing timeline is a finding about you.

THIS IS THE PRE-INTERVIEW ISSUE
  The gate sheet on page 2 stays blank — I mark it by hand in the room. Do not fill it in, do not guess at it, and do not infer those four factors from the CVs.
